// ============================================================================
== 5.1 Letters Are Not Sounds

English is written with twenty-six letters and spoken with about forty-four sounds. The letters are a rough guide at best: *a* is pronounced differently in *cat*, *car*, *call*, *about* and *gate*, and the same sound is spelled *ee* in *see*, *ea* in *sea*, *ie* in *field* and *e* in *me*. Every oral-form question on the paper is built on this gap, and the only defence is to learn words in pairs rather than letters in order.

#concept-box("The vocabulary of the section")[
  *A phoneme* is a sound that changes a word's meaning: /p/ and /b/ are separate phonemes because *pin* and *bin* are different words.

  *A syllable* is one beat of a word, built on one vowel sound.

  *A minimal pair* is two words that differ in exactly one phoneme: *seat* and *sit*, *pin* and *bin*, *thin* and *then*.

  *A homophone* is two words that sound the same and are spelled differently: *bear* and *bare*.

  The exam papers in this section never ask for definitions. They ask you to hear the difference — which is why every rule below is given with example words rather than symbols.
]

// ============================================================================
== 5.2 The Vowels

English has twelve pure vowels and eight glides. The paper's favourite question is not *what is this symbol* but *which word has the same vowel sound as this one* — so learn the contrasts, not the alphabet.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch05_vowels.svg",
  "The twelve pure vowels with an example and the spelling that usually carries each. The fourth column is the contrast the paper tests.", width: 100%)

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch05_diphthongs.svg",
  "The eight gliding vowels. A diphthong begins at one vowel and ends at another, which is why it counts as one syllable.", width: 100%)

#rule-box("The five contrasts worth memorising")[
  + The pair /i:/ and /ɪ/: *seat* and *sit*, *feel* and *fill*, *sheep* and *ship*.
  + The pair /e/ and /æ/: *bed* and *bad*, *pen* and *pan*, *said* and *sad*.
  + The pair /ʊ/ and /u:/: *book* and *food*, *pull* and *pool*, *full* and *fool*.
  + The pair /ɒ/ and /ɔ:/: *hot* and *saw*, *cot* and *caught*, *not* and *naught*.
  + The pair /ʌ/ and /æ/: *cup* and *cap*, *run* and *ran*, *cut* and *cat*.
]

#trap-box("The letter that lies")[
  The letter *a* carries /æ/ in *cat*, /ɑ:/ in *car*, /ɔ:/ in *call*, /ə/ in *about*, /e/ in *many* and /eɪ/ in *gate*. The letter *o* carries /ɒ/ in *hot*, /ʌ/ in *son*, /u:/ in *do*, /əʊ/ in *go* and /ɜ:/ in *word*. A candidate who pronounces the letter rather than the word answers the wrong question — and the paper's examples are chosen from exactly these words.
]

// ============================================================================
== 5.3 The Consonants

Twenty-four consonants, and they are best learned in eight pairs plus eight singles. Each pair differs by one feature only — voicing — and the voicing is the thing the paper tests when it asks which word contains a given sound.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch05_consonants.svg",
  "The eight voiced-voiceless pairs. Put two fingers on your throat: the second sound of each pair buzzes.", width: 100%)

#concept-box("The three sounds that cause the most trouble")[
  *The two th sounds.* /θ/ is voiceless — *think*, *thin*, *three*, *health*. /ð/ is voiced — *this*, *then*, *they*, *breathe*. Nigerian English often replaces both with /t/ or /d/, and the paper asks you to sort them.

  *The pair /ʃ/ and /ʒ/.* /ʃ/ is *ship*, *nation*, *sugar*; /ʒ/ is *measure*, *vision*, *television*. The second is rare and usually appears in the middle of a word.

  *The affricates /tʃ/ and /dʒ/.* *Chair* and *judge*, *watch* and *bridge*. They begin as a stop and end as a hiss, which is why they are written with two letters and counted as one sound.
]

// ============================================================================
== 5.4 Syllables and Silent Letters

A syllable is one vowel sound, not one vowel letter. Counting them is a physical skill: say the word slowly and count the beats, then check that no beat can be dropped.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch05_syllables.svg",
  "How to count the syllables in a word, and why the count matters for the stress questions in Chapter 6.", width: 100%)

#shortcut-box("Silent letters, by the dozen")[
  + *b* before *t* or after *m*: debt, doubt, climb, comb, thumb, lamb.
  + *k* before *n*: know, knee, knife, knock, knot.
  + *w* before *r*: write, wrong, wrist, wrap.
  + *l* before *k*, *m*, *f*: walk, talk, calm, half, salmon.
  + *h* after *r*, *g*, *k*: hour, honest, ghost, khaki.
  + *g* before *n*: sign, design, reign, foreign.
]

// ============================================================================
== 5.5 Worked Examples

#worked-example(
  1,
  "The vowel the paper asks about",
  [Which of the following words contains the same vowel sound as *seat*?

    A. sit \
    B. see \
    C. set \
    D. said],
  [
    *Step 1 — hear the target.* *Seat* is /i:/, a long, tense vowel: the tongue is high and forward and the sound is held.

    *Step 2 — hear each option.* *Sit* is /ɪ/, shorter and looser — the commonest confusion in the section. *See* is /i:/. *Set* is /e/. *Said* is /e/ as well.

    *Step 3 — the pair.* /i:/ and /ɪ/ differ in length and tension, and English uses the difference to separate words: *sheep* and *ship*, *feel* and *fill*, *leave* and *live*.

    *Check.* Say *seat* and then each option in turn, holding the vowel: only *see* can be held as long without changing into another word. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "FOUNDATION",
)

#worked-example(
  2,
  "Counting the syllables",
  [How many syllables are there in the word *comfortable*?

    A. three \
    B. four \
    C. five \
    D. two],
  [
    *Step 1 — say it slowly.* *Comf-ta-ble* — three beats. The written word looks like four (*com-for-ta-ble*), and the difference is the swallowed *or*.

    *Step 2 — the count.* Three vowel sounds: /ʌ/ in *comf*, /ə/ in *ta*, /əl/ in *ble*.

    *Step 3 — the test.* Try dropping a beat: *comf-table* loses a syllable and stops being the word; *com-for-table* adds one that English does not pronounce.

    *Check.* Count the vowel sounds, not the letters. The answer is *A*.
  ],
  tier: "FOUNDATION",
)

#worked-example(
  3,
  "The two th sounds",
  [Which of the following words contains the sound /ð/?

    A. think \
    B. three \
    C. then \
    D. health],
  [
    *Step 1 — voicing.* /ð/ is the voiced *th*: the vocal cords buzz while the tongue sits between the teeth. /θ/ is the same mouth position with no buzz.

    *Step 2 — test each.* *Think*, *three* and *health* are all voiceless — hold your hand to your throat and nothing vibrates. *Then* buzzes.

    *Step 3 — the pattern.* Voiced *th* appears in the little grammatical words — *this*, *that*, *these*, *those*, *then*, *there*, *they*, *them* — and inside verbs such as *breathe* and *bathe*. Voiceless *th* appears in nouns and adjectives such as *thing*, *three*, *health*, *month*.

    *Check.* The buzz decides it, and the pattern confirms it. The answer is *C*.
  ],
  tier: "UTME STANDARD",
)

#worked-example(
  4,
  "The letter that is not pronounced",
  [In which of the following words is the letter *l* silent?

    A. lamp \
    B. walk \
    C. clean \
    D. yellow],
  [
    *Step 1 — the rule.* *l* is silent before *k* (*walk*, *talk*, *chalk*), before *m* (*calm*, *salmon*, *palm*) and before *f* (*half*, *calf*).

    *Step 2 — test each.* *Lamp* — pronounced. *Walk* — /wɔːk/, no *l*. *Clean* — pronounced. *Yellow* — pronounced at the start.

    *Step 3 — the trap.* *Walk* looks as though it should rhyme with *talk*, which is precisely because both have lost the same letter.

    *Check.* Say the word and listen for the /l/; only *walk* has none. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "UTME STANDARD",
)

#worked-example(
  5,
  "Odd one out by sound",
  [Which of the following words does NOT contain the sound /ʌ/?

    A. cup \
    B. son \
    C. put \
    D. blood],
  [
    *Step 1 — the sound.* /ʌ/ is the short, central vowel of *cup* — the mouth relaxed, the tongue in the middle.

    *Step 2 — test each.* *Cup* is /kʌp/. *Son* is /sʌn/ — the *o* is pronounced /ʌ/. *Blood* is /blʌd/ — the *oo* is pronounced /ʌ/. *Put* is /pʊt/, the short *u* of *book*.

    *Step 3 — the pattern.* Spelling is no guide here: *u* in *cup*, *o* in *son* and *oo* in *blood* all give /ʌ/, while *u* in *put* gives /ʊ/. This is the reason the paper uses these four words together.

    *Check.* Three of the four share a sound and one does not; say them and the odd one is audible. The answer is *C*.
  ],
  tier: "JAMB CHALLENGE",
)

