  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 9.2pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[Chapter 5 in one breath]
  #v(3pt)
  #text(size: 10.1pt)[
    English writes twenty-six letters and speaks about forty-four sounds, so the letter is never the evidence. Twelve pure vowels and eight glides supply every vowel, and the paper tests five contrasts: seat and sit, bed and bad, book and food, hot and saw, cup and cap. Twenty-four consonants come in eight voiced-voiceless pairs plus eight singles, and the pairs are decided by voicing — the buzz you can feel at the throat. The two th sounds separate the grammatical words (this, then, they — voiced) from the nouns and adjectives (think, three, health — voiceless). Silent letters hide in bt, kn, wr, lk and gn. A syllable is one vowel sound, not one vowel letter: comfortable has three, queue has one.
  ]
]

== 5.6 Chapter 5 in the Exam Hall — a Two-Minute Set

#mcq(1, [Which word contains the same vowel sound as *seat*?], [sit], [see], [set], [said])

#mcq(2, [Which word contains the sound /θ/?], [this], [then], [think], [they])

#mcq(3, [In which word is the *k* silent?], [kettle], [knife], [skin], [basket])

#mcq(4, [How many syllables are there in *comfortable*?], [three], [four], [five], [two])

#answer-key-grid(("B", "C", "B", "A"))

#answer-notes((
  ("B", [*seat* and *see* are both /i:/, long and tense. A is the short /ɪ/ of *sit*, and C and D are both /e/ — the trap pair for a candidate who hears *seat* as *set*.]),
  ("C", [*think* is voiceless /θ/. A, B and D are the grammatical words with the voiced /ð/, the sound that buzzes at the throat.]),
  ("B", [*k* before *n* is silent: knife, knee, know, knock. A, C and D all pronounce the *k*.]),
  ("A", [*comf-ta-ble*: the *or* is swallowed, so there are three beats. B counts the letters, C counts them twice, and D drops a real syllable.]),
))

